WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:06.110
Based on DC's Hellblazer series, the 2005
comic book flick Constantine became an unlikely

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:10.049
cult favorite despite changing just about
everything from the source material in order

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:11.799
to fit star Keanu Reeves.

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:16.210
Yet despite its popularity, there's still
no sign of a second installment in the franchise.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:19.350
Here's a look at why we never got to see a
Constantine sequel.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.360
Keanu Reeves wasn't interested

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.780
After the original film was released, director
Francis Lawrence said that he wanted to do

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.780
a sequel.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.240
And following the success of his 2007 hit
I Am Legend, he had the clout to make it happen.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.010
Just one problem: Reeves didn't want to do
any sequels, ever, because he said he hadn't

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.149
played any parts interesting enough to revisit.

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:38.789
“God I hate this part.”

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.780
Since then, he's changed his tune, stating
in a 2014 Reddit "Ask Me Anything" session

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:45.780
that

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.780
"I love Constantine, too.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.890
I loved playing John Constantine.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.120
I wish there was a sequel.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.280
With me in it."

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.850
NOW you tell us!

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.269
It wasn't a hit

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:57.210
Most comic book movies seem to fall into one
of two categories: gigantic mega-blockbusters,

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:01.449
like The Avengers, or complete box office
disasters, like Green Lantern.

00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:03.289
"I know, right?!"

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:08.780
But Constantine fell into the middle ground,
earning $230 million worldwide against a $100

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:09.970
million budget.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.790
That's a decent return, but not spectacular
enough to ensure a sequel would get made,

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:17.900
especially since the franchise's R rating
effectively ruled out the teenagers who usually

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.990
make up the target audience for comic book
films.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:24.030
Sure, if Constantine had done Deadpool numbers,
a sequel would have gotten the green light

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.360
on opening weekend.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.360
But it didn't.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.360
“Kay guys!

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.260
Let’s get out there and make a difference!

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.260
You know what to do.”

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.290
The pitch got tossed

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.730
Constantine director Francis Lawrence has
tried a few times to get a sequel off the

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.100
ground, using a script called "Through the
Eye of the Needle" by the screenwriter of

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.170
the original film, Frank A. Cappello.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.630
The plan for the sequel, which reportedly
was based on an original idea rather than

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.170
a story from the comics, was to do a smaller
film with a $35 million budget and make it

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.950
a hard R, which sounds pretty awesome for
a movie about an occult detective.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:54.910
“Fresh meat.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.000
Finger-licking good.”

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.940
Unfortunately, the suits didn't find it as
interesting as Lawrence, and the studio passed.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.170
Maybe it was all just a test?

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:11.009
“It’s only in the face of horror that
you truly find your nobler self.”

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.010
The Constantine TV show flopped

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.780
After plans for the sequel went nowhere, Warner
Bros. decided to go a different direction

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:17.780
with the property.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.390
The result: a Constantine TV series, which
debuted on NBC in 2014.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.420
Starring Matt Ryan, the show took a somewhat
more faithful approach to the character…

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.620
but it was still canceled after just 13 episodes.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.160
The character has since appeared in a guest
role on The CW's Arrow, so even though the

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.810
show is no longer around, the existence of
a competing version of the property still

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.910
muddies the waters for any possible film sequel.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.560
It may be rebooted

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.360
One of the most exciting projects in the DC
Cinematic Universe is the proposed Justice

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:49.209
League Dark movie, which features John Constantine
as part of a group of supernatural types who

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:52.510
tackle mystical threats the regular Justice
League isn't equipped to handle.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.310
That opens a big can of worms in terms of
Constantine 2.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.849
Sure, Reeves might be up for a sequel, but
would he want to play the character as part

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:00.820
of an ensemble superhero movie?

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.900
If not, it's very possible the character will
be recast, making a reboot more likely than

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.900
a sequel.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.740
Sure, Constantine was able to defeat Satan,
but there's one thing even he can't beat:

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.400
Hollywood.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.060
Thanks for watching!

